# Reference verification, batch 10: revisit s001-s040 (Nov 2026)
upd("n631", append="""[Checked: Aristotle, *Metaph.* IV 4, 1006a5–8 (Greek, Perseus/Ross), ‘ἀξιοῦσι δὴ καὶ τοῦτο ἀποδεικνύναι τινὲς διʼ ἀπαιδευσίαν· ἔστι γὰρ ἀπαιδευσία τὸ μὴ γιγνώσκειν τίνων δεῖ ζητεῖν ἀπόδειξιν’. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n643", append="""[Checked: *Phys.* III 1, 201a10–11 (Greek, Perseus/Ross), ‘ἡ τοῦ δυνάμει ὄντος ἐντελέχεια, ᾗ τοιοῦτον, κίνησίς ἐστιν’. The definition is verified at III 1. Gorris’s book number (*6.*, also B *vi.*) is not explained; probably a slip or a reference to the treatment of change in VI.]""", status="probable")
upd("n562", append="""[Checked: *Metaph.* V 7, 1017a35–b2 (Greek, Perseus/Ross), ‘ἔτι τὸ εἶναι σημαίνει καὶ τὸ ὂν τὸ μὲν δυνάμει ῥητὸν τὸ δʼ ἐντελεχείᾳ’, and IX 1, 1045b32–34, ‘τὸ δὲ κατὰ δύναμιν καὶ ἐντελέχειαν’. Both loci verified. A’s *4.* and B’s *ij.* are both wrong.]""", status="verified")
upd("n591", text="""The wording is Peter Lombard’s, not Augustine’s. *Sent.* I d. 8 c. 6 (‘Quod Dei simplicitas nulli praedicamentorum subicitur’) quotes Augustine, *De Trin.* V 1.2 (‘sine qualitate bonum, sine quantitate magnum … Quisquis Deum ita cogitat…’), and comments that ‘illa praedicamenta artis dialecticae Dei naturae minime convenire’. Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 8 q. 3 (Wadding/Vivès), has ‘Contra est Magister in littera, et adducit Augustinum, et ostendit per eum, quod removentur a Deo illa Praedicamenta artis Dialecticae’. Boethius, *De Trin.* c. 4 (‘cuncta mutantur quae praedicari possunt’), expresses the same thought.""", append="""[Checked: Lombard d. 8 c. 6 (PL 192 text, local); Augustine *De Trin.* V 1.2 (augustinus.it, local); Scotus *Ord.* I d. 8 q. 3 (Wadding/Vivès, local); Boethius *De Trin.* c. 4 (la.wikisource, Peiper). Verified as a reference to Augustine via the Master.]""", status="verified")
upd("n598", append="""[Checked: Boethius, *De Trin.* (la.wikisource, Peiper). c. 4 distinguishes predication ‘secundum rem’ from predication ‘quasi circumstantias rei’; c. 6 ends ‘Ita igitur substantia continet unitatem, relatio multiplicat trinitatem’. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n639", append="""[Checked: Gregory, *Moralia* XXIX c. 1 n. 1 (PL 76, la.wikisource *Moralia/VI*): ‘Dominus Deus noster Jesus Christus in eo quod virtus et sapientia Dei est, de Patre ante tempora natus est; vel potius quia nec coepit nasci, nec desiit, dicamus verius semper natus. Non autem possumus dicere semper nascitur, ne imperfectus videatur’. The passage ends ‘Unde bene nunc ad beatum Job dicitur:’ and c. 2 opens ‘VERS. 12. Nunquid post ortum tuum praecepisti diluculo’. Gorris’s correction to Job 38:12 is verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n534", append="""[Checked: Hilary, *De Trin.* (PL 10, local, books I–XII) was searched for *caritas/dilectio* with *natura*. The closest passage is IX 61: ‘Nescit autem Deus aliud aliquando quam dilectio esse, neque aliud quam pater esse. Et qui diligit, non invidet: et qui pater est, non etiam non pater totus est’, with the heading ‘ut Pater, nil non dat quod habet’. This is probably what is meant: the Father gives all by love and by being Father. *XV* would then be a misreading of *IX*, which is not certain.]""", status="probable")
upd("n555", text="""Augustine, *De Trin.* V 5.6: ‘Pater non dicitur Pater nisi ex eo quod est ei Filius’. Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 7 q. 1 (Wadding/Vivès), opens with ‘Augustinus 5. de Trinit. cap. 6. “eo Pater est, quo est ei Filius”’. The book is V, not XV; A and B (*xv.*) both err.""", append="""[Checked: Augustine *De Trin.* V (augustinus.it, local) and Scotus *Ord.* I d. 7 q. 1 (Wadding/Vivès, local). Corrected and verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n252", text="""Giles of Rome, *In I Sent.* d. 1 (art. 1 on the power that elicits enjoyment). The printed commentary does not support the view reported here. It concludes ‘sola voluntas per se, et proprie frui dicetur’ and ‘frui non est actus intellectus, sed voluntatis’, allowing only that natural happiness lies in the intellect. The report may follow a different redaction or a secondary source.""", append="""[Checked: Giles, *In I Sent.* (Córdoba 1699, local OCR), d. 1, the article beginning ‘videtur quod frui sit actus intellectus’. The attribution is not confirmed.]""", status="uncertain")
upd("n656", append="""[Checked: Scotus, *Ord.* I d. 10 q. un. (Wadding/Vivès, local) opens with other arguments (the definition of *natura*; Averroes, *Phys.* VIII comm. 46) and cites no Augustine. Still open.]""")
upd("n355", text="""Francis of Meyronnes († c. 1328). His *Conflatus*, Prologue q. 6 (‘Utrum ens quidditative dicatur de divina essentia ultimate praecisa’), concludes: ‘Ad quaestionem igitur dico quod ens non dicitur in quid de divina essentia’. He also says ‘probatum est evidenter quod ens non dicitur quidditative, sed denominative de divina essentia’. He then distinguishes two kinds of denominative predication: ‘Una quae accipitur a posteriori et ab accidente … sicut a iustitia dicitur iustus. Alia est quae accipitur ab essentialiter posteriori, et ista potest esse univoca’. This is exactly Gorris’s ‘denominativa licet non denominativa accidentalis’. B confirms *Franci.* This is the first named Scotist after Scotus in Gorris’s text.""", append="""[Checked: Franciscus de Mayronis, *Preclarissima ac multum subtilia egregiaque scripta* (Venice 1520; archive.org bub_gb_QJ4loltkxYEC), fols. 5v–6r, read from the page images because the OCR is unusable. Abbreviations expanded. Verified.]""", status="verified")
upd("n447", append="""[Checked: Ockham’s *Reportatio* II qq. 12–13 (OTh V, 1981) is not freely online and was not opened, so the *nominales* attribution is still unchecked. Gorris’s reply to the *Eth.* II argument has an exact parallel in Aquinas, *ST* I-II q. 94 a. 1 ad 1 (bkv.unifr.ch): ‘philosophus intendit ibi investigare genus virtutis … illa tantum ponit quae sunt principia humanorum actuum’. Still open as to Ockham.]""")
upd("n354", append="""[Checked: Avicenna, *Opera* (Venice 1508; archive.org auiceneperhypate00avic and bub_gb_JJThV3vtDJIC) was located, but its OCR is too corrupt to search, and the *Philosophia prima* was not read page by page. Still open.]""")
upd("n420", append="""[Checked: Averroes, *De anima* with commentary (Venice 1516; archive.org hin-wel-all-00001468-001) and Avicenna, *Opera* (Venice 1508) were located. In both the Gothic OCR is unusable (no *arca/archa* hit), and III comm. 6 was not read from the page images. Still open.]""")
